sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch12-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Most spiral Foraminifera are plane (discoid) spirals because some force keeps the chambers in a plane, especially in forms that live attached to rock or seaweed, whose whorls are flattened on the attached surface; freely floating forms such as Globigerina tend towards helicoid spirals.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Attached Foraminifera form plane spirals with whorls flattened on the attached side";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XII, “The Spiral Shells of the Foraminifera”; topic “Types of foraminiferal shell”. Scope: benthic attached versus planktonic Foraminifera"
}
